          "term": "hiv prevention",
          "heading": false
        }
      ],
      "extract": "Following the WHO recommendation in September 2015 that “oral pre-exposure prophylaxis (PrEP) should be offered as an additional prevention choice for people at substantial risk of HIV infection as part of combination HIV prevention approaches”, partners in countries expressed the need for practical advice on how to consider the introduction of PrEP and start implementation. In response, WHO has developed this",
      "extractCut": true
    },
    {
      "id": "sec-001-the-counsellors-module",
      "number": null,
      "title": "THE COUNSELLORS MODULE",
      "level": 1,
      "pageStart": 6,
      "pageEnd": 6,
      "labelStart": "4",
      "labelEnd": "4",
      "words": 161,
      "summary": null,
      "keywords": [
        {
          "term": "counsel prep",
          "heading": false
        },
        {
          "term": "prep users",
          "heading": false
        },
        {
          "term": "module",
          "heading": true
        },
        {
          "term": "community education",
          "heading": false
        },
        {
          "term": "prevention choice",
          "heading": false
        }
      ],
      "extract": "This module is for staff in clinical and other settings who counsel PrEP users. Those who counsel PrEP users may be lay, peer or professional counsellors and healthcare workers including nurses, clinical officers and doctors. This module does not include information about community education; that is the focus of the module for community educators and advocates. Community education can shape awareness of and demand",
      "extractCut": true
    },
    {
      "id": "sec-002-counselling-goals-related-to-prep",
      "number": null,
      "title": "Counselling goals related to PrEP",
      "level": 2,
      "pageStart": 6,
      "pageEnd": 7,
      "labelStart": "4",
      "labelEnd": "5",
      "words": 276,
      "summary": null,
      "keywords": [
        {
          "term": "prep",
          "heading": true
        },
        {
          "term": "counselling",
          "heading": true
        },
        {
          "term": "follow-up counselling",
          "heading": false
        },
        {
          "term": "sexual health",
          "heading": false
        },
        {
          "term": "goals",
          "heading": true
        },
        {
          "term": "current sexual",
          "heading": false
        },
        {
          "term": "harm reduction",
          "heading": false
        },
        {
          "term": "side-effects",
          "heading": false
        }
      ],
      "extract": "The goals of counselling depend on whether the client is starting PrEP or is already using PrEP. Counselling may explore current sexual health and drug use protection plans and raise awareness about PrEP and other prevention strategies. Mixing sexual health education with client-centred discussion (see below) can facilitate open communication about sexual health desires, goals and plans. Counsellors can help clients",
      "extractCut": true
    },
    {
      "id": "sec-003-prep-and-seasons-of-risk",
      "number": null,
      "title": "PrEP and “seasons of risk”",
      "level": 2,
      "pageStart": 7,
      "pageEnd": 7,
      "labelStart": "5",
      "labelEnd": "5",
      "words": 375,
      "summary": null,
      "keywords": [
        {
          "term": "prep",
          "heading": true
        },
        {
          "term": "seasons",
          "heading": true
        },
        {
          "term": "stop prep",
          "heading": false
        },
        {
          "term": "prep users",
          "heading": false
        },
        {
          "term": "risky situations",
          "heading": false
        },
        {
          "term": "prompt person",
          "heading": false
        },
        {
          "term": "risk",
          "heading": true
        },
        {
          "term": "relationship",
          "heading": false
        }
      ],
      "extract": "People often move in and out of “seasons of risk” and “risky situations” for HIV infection. For both antiretroviral therapy (ART) for HIV treatment and PrEP, adherence is crucial. However, adherence to ART involves lifelong therapy with daily doses. In contrast, among PrEP users, HIV exposure may be episodic. People often move in and out of “seasons of risk” and “risky situations” (1, 2). Thus, people taking PrEP",
      "extractCut": true
    },
    {
      "id": "sec-004-who-can-counsel-about-prep",
      "number": null,
      "title": "Who can counsel about PrEP?",
      "level": 2,
      "pageStart": 7,
      "pageEnd": 8,
      "labelStart": "5",
      "labelEnd": "6",
      "words": 470,
      "summary": null,
      "keywords": [
        {
          "term": "lay counsellors",
          "heading": false
        },
        {
          "term": "peer counsellors",
          "heading": false
        },
        {
          "term": "prep",
          "heading": true
        },
        {
          "term": "lay providers",
          "heading": false
        },
        {
          "term": "prep counselling",
          "heading": false
        },
        {
          "term": "trained counsellor",
          "heading": false
        },
        {
          "term": "counselling skills",
          "heading": false
        },
        {
          "term": "refresher training",
          "heading": false
        }
      ],
      "extract": "Counsellors and health workers should be carefully trained on PrEP use before they counsel clients about PrEP. In addition, the user’s preferences should be considered. Some users may prefer talking with a peer or lay counsellor, who has some shared experiences. Others may prefer counselling from a medically trained counsellor (for example, a nurse, doctor or clinical officer). Integrating training on PrEP",
      "extractCut": true
    },
    {
      "id": "sec-005-counselling-strategies",
      "number": null,
      "title": "Counselling strategies",
      "level": 2,
      "pageStart": 8,
      "pageEnd": 15,
      "labelStart": "6",
